{{also|UG|Ug|.ug}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʌɡ/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|ugge}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|uggr||fear, apprehension, dread}}, related to {{cog|non|ógn||terror, threat, dispute}} and {{m|non|agi||terror, strife, fear, punishment}}. More at {{l|en|awe}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ugg}}, {{l|en|oug}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} A feeling of [[fear]], [[horror]] or [[disgust]].
#: {{usex|en|He took an '''ug''' at's meht.}}<!-- as used in The English Dialect Dictionary‎ -->
# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} An object of [[disgust]].
#: {{usex|en|What an '''ug''' ye've myed yorsel.}}<!-- as used in The English Dialect Dictionary‎ -->
# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} [[vomit|Vomited]] matter.
# {{lb|en|Northumbria}} A [[surfeit]].

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|fear; horror}} {{l|en|dread}}, {{l|en|fright}}; see also [[Thesaurus:fear]]
* {{sense|digust}} {{l|en|distaste}}, {{l|en|loathsomeness}}, {{l|en|revulsion}}
* {{sense|object of disgust}} {{l|en|abomination}}
* {{sense|vomit}} {{l|en|chunder}}, {{l|en|sick}}; see also [[Thesaurus:vomit]]
* {{sense|surfeit}} {{l|en|glut}}, {{l|en|surplus}}; see also [[Thesaurus:excess]]

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|ugfou}}
* {{l|en|uggin}}
* {{l|en|ugsome}}
* {{l|en|ugsomely}}
* {{l|en|ugsomeness}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|ugly}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|ug|g|ed}}

# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} To feel [[abhorrence]] or [[disgust]].
#* {{quote-book
|year=1822
|title=Poems, Chiefly in the Scottish Dialect
|first=Robert|last=Wilson
|chapter=Answer to an Epistle from a Friend
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iJxgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71
|page=71
|passage=Wha weds a cankert thriftless wife, / Weds to his days eternal strife, / For, like the Tron-Kirk bell, / She ever hammers on his lugs, / Till her an' hame at last he '''uggs''' / As the dire door o' hell!
}}
# {{lb|en|North England|and|Scotland|obsolete}} To [[vomit]].

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|feel abhorrence}} {{l|en|abhor}}, {{l|en|loathe}}; see also [[Thesaurus:hate]].  Alternatively: {{l|en|nauseate}}, {{l|en|sicken}}.
* {{sense|vomit}} {{l|en|heave}}, {{l|en|pray to the porcelain god}}, {{l|en|throw up}}; see also [[Thesaurus:regurgitate]]

